e-field circuit objects, and establishes the assembly bound with denominator $p^4$ under its stated challenge assumptions. An executable Rust prover accompanies the model. Creusot/Why3 contracts provide a partial implementation connection, and a conditional theorem relates successful verifier traces to the model event under an undischarged value-correspondence premise. Neither the transcript's online challenge distribution nor a multi-round Fiat--Shamir reduction is established. The contribution is the composition, within one proof assistant, of compiler correctness with a GKR assembly model, together with an explicit account of the remaining implementation and cryptographic obligations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2505.00554] Protocols for Univariate Sumcheck (https://vpn.luca-server.com/px/aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmcvYWJzLzI1MDUuMDA1NTQ)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; [Submitted on 1 May 2025 (v1), last revised 21 Mar 2026 (this version, v5)] ... The other two are reductions from univariate domain identity and univariate sumcheck to multivariate evaluation, respectively, and each can be combined with Gemini (Bootle et al., Eurocrypt 2022). ...    | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.00554 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite ... [v2] Wed, 8 Oct 2025 16:29:18 UTC (22 KB)\n\n# Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\n\n[Submitted on 1 May 2025 (v1), last revised 21 Mar 2026 (this version, v5)]\n\n# Title:Protocols for Univariate Sumcheck\n\nAuthors:Malcom Mohamed\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Protocols for Univariate Sumcheck, by Malcom Mohamed\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Three candidate approaches for univariate sumcheck over roots of unity are presented. The first takes the form of a multilinear evaluation protocol, which can be combined with the standard multivariate sumcheck protocol. The other two are reductions from univariate domain identity and univariate sumcheck to multivariate evaluation, respectively, and each can be combined with Gemini (Bootle et al., Eurocrypt 2022). Optionally, natural round reductions from $m$ to $\\log(m)$ or $O(\\sqrt{m})$ are supported, while retaining linear prover time.\n\nComments:  | To appear in APKC@AsiaCCS'26. Full version\nSubjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2505.00554 [cs.CR]\n   | (or arXiv:2505.00554v5 [cs.CR] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.00554 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Malcom Mohamed [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 1 May 2025 14:29:32 UTC (16 KB)\n[v2] Wed, 8 Oct 2025 16:29:18 UTC (22 KB)\n[v3] Fri, 19 Dec 2025 16:45:49 UTC (17 KB)\n[v4] Thu, 29 Jan 2026 13:59:30 UTC (22 KB)\n[v5] Sat, 21 Mar 2026 11:02:24 UTC (26 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhekate-math 0.12.1 - Docs.rs (https://docs.rs/crate/hekate-math/0.12.1/builds)\nciteturn9search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; For Sumcheck, GKR-based provers, and Binius protocols. ...   * rustc 1.101.0-nightly (32dba69d6 2026-10-09)docsrs 0.0.0 (345207b5bd3e08551abe806ac963257c692997f6 2026-10-08 )Oct 10, 2026 took 8s 498.1 MB\n# hekate-math 0.12.1\n\nFormally verified binary tower fields (F(2^k) to F(2^256)), constant-time by default, with hardware carry-less multiplication. For Sumcheck, GKR-based provers, and Binius protocols.\n\n#### Builds\n\n  * rustc 1.101.0-nightly (32dba69d6 2026-10-09)docsrs 0.0.0 (345207b5bd3e08551abe806ac963257c692997f6 2026-10-08 )Oct 10, 2026 took 8s 498.1 MB\n\n#### hekate-math's sandbox limits\n\nAll the builds on docs.rs are executed inside a sandbox with limited resources. The limits for this crate are the following:\n\nAvailable RAM  | 6.4 GB\nMaximum rustdoc execution time  | 15m\nMaximum size of a build log  | 102.4 kB\nNetwork access  | blocked\nMaximum number of build targets  | 10\n\nIf a build fails because it hit one of those limits please open an issue to get them increased.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2609.37021)\nciteturn9search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; arXiv:2610.37319 ... Submitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nAnalogScout: Simulation-Efficient PVT-Aware Analog Sizing with LLM Agents\n\nYichen Long, Keren Zhu\n\narXiv:2610.37319\n\nAuthors: Yichen Long, Keren Zhu\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nAnalog device sizing must satisfy all specifications across process, voltage, and temperature (PVT) corners to ensure tape-out success, but simulating every candidate at all corners dominates the sizing time and slows design iteration. Conventional surrogate-based optimizers search the sizing space directly, while recent large language model (LLM) agents add circuit knowledge to designs but still delegate the sizes to global optimizers. Both learn the circuit response from repeated multi-corner SPICE simulations and optimize on top of it, which becomes expensive under a limited simulation budget. Providing an agent with reusable verified designs and sizing sub-blocks analytically could save most of this budget. We propose AnalogScout, a simulation-efficient PVT-aware sizing framework driven by an LLM agent. \nByonggon Chun\n\narXiv:2610.38329\n\nAuthors: Byonggon Chun\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nFrontier large language models write correct CUDA kernels for most single-operator benchmark tasks, yet their accuracy collapses on accelerators whose architecture and programming language they have rarely seen. We present a case study of an LLM agent loop built for such accelerators. Our example is the FuriosaAI RNGD NPU, programmed in a recent, sparsely documented Rust DSL, where the loop optimized three Gemma-4-12B decode kernels over 12 days on a shared, scarce device. The loop builds its own target knowledge: it writes and checks a summary of the DSL documentation, measures the chip with microbenchmarks, records compiler-derived legality rules, and revisits this knowledge when the toolchain changes, rechecking rules and measurements where it can. A role-based search over per-kernel Design Trees explores whole-kernel designs and accumulates small accepted changes in the best version of each kernel. Because device time is scarce and readings are noisy, cheap checks screen new designs before any device run, and late in the run a change was accepted only after repeated comparisons with the best kernel on the device. The final kernels reach a 9.293x geomean speedup over the official baseline, each within 1.25x of the cycles needed just to move its weights. Our prompts, profiler and acceptance test are available at https://github.com/bgchun-fs/multi-agent-kernel-gen-loop.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsumcheck package - github.com/consensys/gnark/std/recursion/sumcheck - Go Packages (https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/consensys/gnark%40v0.16.4/std/recursion/sumcheck)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # sumcheck ... Go to latest Published: Oct 7, 2026 License: Apache-2.0\n\n# sumcheck\n\npackage Image\n\nVersion: v0.16.4\n\nOpens a new window with list of versions in this module.\n\nLatest Latest Image: Warning\n\nThis package is not in the latest version of its module.\n\nGo to latest Published: Oct 7, 2026 License: Apache-2.0\n\n## Details\n\n  * Image: checked Valid go.mod file Image\n\nThe Go module system was introduced in Go 1.11 and is the official dependency management solution for Go.\n\n  * Image: checked Redistributable license Image\n\nRedistributable licenses place minimal restrictions on how software can be used, modified, and redistributed.\n\n  * Image: checked Tagged version Image\n\nModules with tagged versions give importers more predictable builds.\n\n  * Image: unchecked Stable version Image\n\nWhen a project reaches major version v1 it is considered stable.\n\n## Repository\n\ngithub.com/consensys/gnark\n\n## Links\n\n  * Open Source Insights\n    \n    g_i(X) = ∑_{x_j ∈ {0,1}, j ≥ i+1} g((r_j)_{j < i}, X, (x_j)_{j ≥ i+1}).\n    \n\nThe verifier checks that gᵢ(r_i) = gᵢ₊₁(0) + gᵢ₊₁(1) for every round. After the last round the verifier has to evaluate g((r_j){1 ≤ j ≤ n}) on its own.\n\nTo allow for incorporating the sumcheck protocol inside a larger GKR protocol, parallel verification and different types of function g, the protocol instead defines LazyClaims interface which defines the aspects of the claimable function:\n\n  * the number of parallel proofs being verified (method LazyClaims.NbClaims) and the combined folded evaluation (method [LazyClaims.CominedSum]).\n  * the number of variables the function takes (method LazyClaims.NbVars)\n  * the degree of the univariate polynomial for the i-th variable (method LazyClaims.Degree)\n  * how to perform the final evaluation of the function (method LazyClaims.AssertEvaluation):\n  * the claim can directly evaluate the function itself (plain sumcheck),\n  * the evaluation is deferred as an input to another sumcheck protocol run (as in GKR),\n  * by opening a multivariate polynomial opening by letting prover to provide the opening proof.\n\nThe package is still work in progress. We do not yet expose prover for creating the sumcheck proofs but aim to integrate the proof creation to be automatic given the specific claim type.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08258)\nciteturn9academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Date: Tue Oct  6 12:35:49 2026 ... We build on sumcheck- and lookup-based arguments to certify Transformer computations, while supporting next-token loss and task-specific audit objectives.\nTitle: zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models\nAuthors: Junkai Liang, Zhanpeng Guo, Pengfei Wu, Qingni Shen, Jiaheng Zhang, Zhonghai Wu, Haiyang Xue, Shengfang Zhai\nDate: Tue Oct  6 12:35:49 2026\n\nAuditing the claimed outcomes of large language model (LLM) training is challenging when model weights and training data are private, while cryptographically proving the full training process is prohibitively expensive at Transformer scale. We present zkLLMPoT, a zero-knowledge framework that certifies auditor-defined properties of a trained checkpoint through forward evaluation rather than verification of its optimization trajectory. zkLLMPoT includes 2 phases: 1) The trainer fixes the architecture and the model weights are committed. Then the auditor selects challenge sequences, preventing the trainer from modifying the checkpoint in response to the audit data. 2) Then the trainer proves the objective value attained by the committed model on those sequences. This formulation makes the certification cost independent of the number of training iterations, without revealing model weights or requiring access to private training data. We build on sumcheck- and lookup-based arguments to certify Transformer computations, while supporting next-token loss and task-specific audit objectives. Across four model families, operator-level benchmarks yield proving times of 41-59 seconds for 1.1-1.5B-parameter models and 131 seconds at 13B for the covered operators, with verification below half a second at a sequence length of 512.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · PolyhedraZK/Expander · GitHub (https://github.com/PolyhedraZK/Expander/releases)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * Adding sumcheck-cuda by @slzk in #131 ... Image: @zhenfeizhang zhenfeizhang released this 15 Oct 02:43\n# Choose a tag to compare\n\n## Sorry, something went wrong.\n\nFilter\n\nLoading [Input]\n\n## Sorry, something went wrong.\